            <ArticleTitle>Discrepancy between coronary angiography and autopsy finding.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>247-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/PAF.0b013e3181dd5ba1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This article presents the case of a 71-year-old man who developed septic shock complicated by acute renal failure. Because of clinical suspicion of myocardial infarction, 24 hours before passing away, the patient had a coronary cineangiography. Limited incidences and views have been used to avoid contrast-induced nephropathy and no significant lesions were founded. However, the autopsy revealed significant stenosis of coronary arteries. This case report discusses the paradox of this finding.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
